Two request threads call `incrementRequestCount()` at the same time. Each reads the same value before either writes it back, so one increment quietly vanishes.

```java
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class RequestCounterService {
    private int requestCount = 0; // shared mutable state

    // UNSAFE: requestCount++ is read-modify-write
    public void incrementRequestCount() {
        requestCount++; // three steps: read, add, write
    }

    public int getRequestCount() {
        return requestCount;
    }
}
```

```java
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class RequestCounterService {
    // SAFE: one atomic compare-and-swap per increment
    private final AtomicInteger requestCount = new AtomicInteger(0);

    public void incrementRequestCount() {
        requestCount.incrementAndGet(); // atomic, no lost updates
    }

    public int getRequestCount() {
        return requestCount.get();
    }
}
```
*`AtomicInteger` runs the read-modify-write as a single atomic CPU instruction, so no update is ever lost — even under thousands of concurrent threads.*

Five worker threads wait for tasks. `notify()` wakes only one, and the JVM may keep waking the same thread, so the other four starve.

```java
import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.Queue;

public class TaskQueue {
    private final Queue<Runnable> tasks = new LinkedList<>();
    private final Object lock = new Object();

    public void addTask(Runnable task) {
        synchronized (lock) {
            tasks.add(task);
            lock.notify(); // PROBLEM: wakes only ONE waiting thread
        }
    }

    public Runnable getTask() throws InterruptedException {
        synchronized (lock) {
            while (tasks.isEmpty()) {
                lock.wait(); // many workers wait here
            }
            return tasks.poll();
        }
    }
}
```

```java
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class FairCounter {
    // true = fair: threads acquire in first-in, first-out order
    private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock(true);
    private long count = 0;

    public void increment() {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            count++;
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }
}
```
*Use `lock.notifyAll()` to wake every waiter, and `new ReentrantLock(true)` to serve threads in FIFO order so no thread is starved indefinitely.*

`count++` compiles to load, add, store. Two threads interleave those instructions, so increments overwrite each other.

```java
public class VisitorCounter {
    private int count = 0; // shared mutable field

    // UNSAFE: compiles to load count, add 1, store count
    public void incrementVisitor() {
        count++;
    }

    public int getCount() {
        return count;
    }
}
```

```java
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class VisitorCounter {
    private final AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger(0);

    // SAFE: incrementAndGet is one atomic compare-and-swap
    public void incrementVisitor() {
        count.incrementAndGet();
    }

    public int getCount() {
        return count.get();
    }
}
```
*`AtomicInteger.incrementAndGet()` performs read-modify-write as a single atomic operation, so no thread can interleave in the middle.*

When the lock order follows the call, two opposite transfers can wait on each other forever.

```java
public class BankAccount {
    private final long id;
    private int balance;

    public BankAccount(long id, int balance) {
        this.id = id;
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    // DEADLOCK PRONE: lock order varies with the call
    public void transfer(BankAccount to, int amount) {
        synchronized (this) {        // locks source first
            synchronized (to) {      // then destination
                this.balance -= amount;
                to.balance += amount;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```java
    // DEADLOCK SAFE: one consistent global lock order
    public void transfer(BankAccount to, int amount) {
        BankAccount first  = this.id < to.id ? this : to;
        BankAccount second = this.id < to.id ? to : this;
        synchronized (first) {
            synchronized (second) {
                this.balance -= amount;
                to.balance += amount;
            }
        }
    }
```
*A consistent lock order breaks Circular Wait — no two threads can ever wait on each other in a cycle.*

```java
public class LivelockDemo {
    static volatile boolean aYields = true;
    static volatile boolean bYields = true;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Each thread keeps stepping aside for the other -> active, but no progress
        Runnable a = () -> {
            while (bYields) { Thread.onSpinWait(); } // busy-waiting, not blocked
            System.out.println("A proceeds");
        };
        Runnable b = () -> {
            while (aYields) { Thread.onSpinWait(); }
            System.out.println("B proceeds");
        };
        new Thread(a).start();
        new Thread(b).start();
    }
}
```

In deadlock both threads sit idle in BLOCKED. In livelock both run flat out yet neither finishes — fix it by breaking symmetry with randomized backoff or lock ordering.

Without a happens-before edge, Thread B can see `configLoaded` as true while `config` is still null, due to reordering or stale cache.

```java
import java.util.Properties;

public class ConfigurationLoader {
    private boolean configLoaded = false; // NOT volatile
    private Properties config;

    public void loadConfig() {
        config = loadPropertiesFromFile(); // step 1
        configLoaded = true;               // step 2 may be seen first
    }

    public String getProperty(String key) {
        if (configLoaded) {                // might see true
            return config.getProperty(key); // but config might be null
        }
        return null;
    }

    private Properties loadPropertiesFromFile() { return new Properties(); }
}
```

```java
import java.util.Properties;

public class ConfigurationLoader {
    private volatile boolean configLoaded = false; // volatile flag
    private Properties config;

    public void loadConfig() {
        config = loadPropertiesFromFile();
        configLoaded = true; // all prior writes are now published
    }

    public String getProperty(String key) {
        if (configLoaded) {                // reads see the published writes
            return config.getProperty(key);
        }
        return null;
    }

    private Properties loadPropertiesFromFile() { return new Properties(); }
}
```
*A `volatile` write happens-before every later read of it, so all writes made before setting `configLoaded` are visible once another thread sees it as true.*
